Apologies: the transcription above is incomplete and the structure is wrong. The page has far more text than I recorded. Here is the corrected, full transcription of the printed page.

<page type="text">
  <layout>One page of text in a single column; in the left margin, a marginal note block beside the lower half of the text (side="left"). Page number 36 printed above the text, at the top right of the column.</layout>
  <page_number>36</page_number>
  <main_text>
    <p>ex differente massâ. Contrà enim est hy-pothesis Apostoli: quia ex eâdem prōfus massâ facit figulus vasa, pro arbitrio suo &amp; potestate. Neque enim debet massæ differentiam iſtam. Quare injuriam illi nō faceret; ſi tantùm vasa dedecoris, facere, aut honoris. Ita Deus juſtitiam non violat, quomodocunque agat: omnium miſerendo; aut omnes indurando; aut denique aliquorum miſerendo, aliquos indurando; &amp; eos quidem, quos vult. Neque enim ulli quicquam debet: non majori; non minori. Proinde, de debito &amp; juſtitiâ, aliena hîc eſt conſideratio.</p>
    <p><note side="left">4. De Vaſis ad interitum parati, ibid. v.22.</note>Quartus locus fit <i>ille periculose</i> [Greek: ſpecial text, not reliably legible] à quibusdam: [Greek] <i>eis apōleian</i>, v. 22. credo, ob explicationes Pelagianas, humanęq; rationis, recedentes nō ſolùm à litera; ſed etiam à mente contextus. A quo enim vasa ſunt præparata; niſi à figulo, Deo? Cur vaſa iræ; niſi ad interitū? quemadmodum miſericordiæ, in gloriam. v.23. pro poteſtate, quam habet figulus, in eandem maſſam.</p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Corrections and doubtful readings:
- The Greek phrases in the "Quartus locus" paragraph (ſpecial text after "ille periculose", and the Greek before "à quibusdam") are printed Greek. They are not legible enough for me to copy letter by letter, so I have not reproduced them; they should be checked against the scan.
- "ſpecial" is a placeholder for the Greek words; the Greek letters in the first Greek phrase and the second read as Greek with accents, not transcribable with confidence.
- The forms "Vaſis", "vaſa" and "eâdem" follow the printed spelling; "ſolùm" and "ſi" keep the long s as printed.
- The first line of the page ("ex differente massâ") begins with a fragment of the previous sentence and is therefore not marked cont.
- The page number "36" is printed at the top right, with a small asterisk-like mark beside it.
- The right-hand edge (a column of numbered letters and a stamp) belongs to the adjoining page and is not transcribed.
- Words I could not read cleanly, which may need checking: "eis apōleian" (the Greek transliteration is my reading), and "vasa iræ" (possibly "vasa iræ"), both marked here for review.</notes>
</page>